            <ArticleTitle>Committee opinion no. 591: challenges for overweight and obese women.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>726-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/01.AOG.0000444457.29401.a0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Overweight and obesity are epidemic in the United States. Obesity is a risk factor for numerous conditions, including diabetes, hypertension, high cholesterol, stroke, heart disease, certain types of cancer, and arthritis. The prevalence of obesity is high, exceeding 30% in adult women and men. Many women, irrespective of demographic characteristics or income, are vulnerable to becoming overweight or obese because of limited resources for physical activity and healthy food choices, work commitments, and family demands. Clinicians and public health officials should address not only individual behavior but also the built environment in their efforts to reduce overweight and obesity in their patient populations.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
